# ORDERING IS LOAD-BEARING — read before editing this file.
#
# git applies the LAST matching pattern for a given attribute, so the
# catch-all `* text=auto` MUST come first and every specific override MUST
# follow it. It used to sit in the middle of this file, below the `*.pdf
# binary` rule, which meant the catch-all silently WON for .pdf: `git
# check-attr text -- foo.pdf` reported `text: auto`, not `unset`.
#
# The damage that caused (found 2026-08-02, in a fresh worktree):
#   - On checkout with core.autocrlf=true, 69 binary fixtures were
#     CRLF-inflated. A PDF's cross-reference table stores ABSOLUTE BYTE
#     OFFSETS, so every offset past the first rewrite pointed at the wrong
#     byte and the file failed to parse.
#   - Worse, four fixtures were damaged IN THE INDEX ITSELF, because git
#     stripped their CRs at `git add` time rather than at checkout:
#     hello.pdf (703 bytes committed vs 709 true), minimal.pdf (331 vs 335),
#     xref-eol/entry-crlf.pdf (551 vs 556), xref-eol/struct-crlf.pdf
#     (551 vs 563). Those last two exist SPECIFICALLY to test CRLF handling
#     in cross-reference sections (ISO 32000-1 §7.5.4 requires exactly
#     20-byte xref entries) — normalization turned six entries into 19
#     bytes, so the very bytes under test were the bytes destroyed.
#
# Neither state worked: the index form failed with BadEntry, the smudged
# form with NotAnXrefSection. The main development tree only kept passing
# because it still held the original on-disk bytes and had never been
# re-checked-out.
#
# So: catch-all first, specific rules after. Do not reorder.
* text=auto

# Force LF for anything that must run as a script or gets parsed
# line-sensitively, regardless of the checkout platform's autocrlf
# setting — a CRLF-mangled shebang script fails to run.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.rs text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Markdown/docs: normalize to LF too, for consistent diffs across
# Windows/Linux/macOS contributors — content doesn't depend on it,
# but mixed line endings make diffs noisy.
*.md text eol=lf

# Binary test fixtures and assets: NEVER apply end-of-line normalization.
# A PDF's cross-reference table stores absolute byte offsets; if git
# rewrote CRLF<->LF inside a committed .pdf on checkout, every offset past
# the first rewrite would be wrong and the file would fail to parse. Mark
# these binary explicitly so `* text=auto` below can never misdetect a
# NUL-free synthetic PDF as text and mangle it. (`binary` == `-text -diff`.)
*.pdf binary
*.bin binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.p12 binary
*.pfx binary

# Font binaries (decision 004 §10 item 4): bundled/fixture font files
# must never pass through EOL normalization — same rationale as the
# PDF-fixture rules above.
*.cff binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.pfb binary

# Vendored OCRcer (tools/sync-ocrcer.py): byte-exact copies of OCRcer HEAD,
# compared byte-for-byte by tools/check-ocrcer-vendored.py -- never convert.
vendor/** -text
crates/pdfcer-core/src/ocr/engine_ocrcer.rs -text
